==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|lateste}}, from {{inh|en|ang|latost}}, {{m|ang|latest}}, {{m|ang|lætest}}, superlative of {{m|ang|læt}}, whence {{cog|en|late}}. {{dbt|en|last}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈleɪ.tɪst/|a=UK,US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-latest.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪtɪst|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{head|en|superlative adjective}}

# {{en-superlative of|late}}
# {{lb|en|now|_|rare|poetic}} [[last|Last]], [[final]].
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|II|i|passage=Whiles the sad pang approching she does feele, / Brayes out her '''latest''' breath, and vp her eyes doth seele.}}
# Most [[recent]].
#: {{syn|en|du jour|q1=formal}}
#: {{ux|en|Here is the '''latest''' news on the accident.}}
#: {{ux|en|My '''latest''' album, which is being published next week, is even better than my last one.}}
#: {{ux|en|It's the '''latest''' fashion, ma'am.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1955|month=April|author=Cecil J. Allen|title=British Locomotive Practice and Performance|journal=Railway Magazine|page=261|text=Equally I can stand in a station like Lucerne in Switzerland, and watch with ill-concealed admiration the majestic entry of one of the '''latest''' Gotthard "Ae 6/6" electrics, packing 6,000 horsepower within its 60 ft. of length and 121 tons of weight, with its sleek dark green sides and handsome stainless steel bands and front wings.}}

